===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|pen-enlosure-nailed-pinned}}
{{wp|+ (enclosure)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|pen}}, {{m|enm|penne|t=enclosure for animals}}, from {{inh|en|ang|penn|t=enclosure, fold, pen}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*pennō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*pannijō|t=pin, bolt, nail, tack}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bend-|t=pointed peg, nail, edge}}. Related to {{m|en|pin}}.

Sense “prison” originally figurative extension to “enclosure for persons” (1845), later influenced by {{m|en|penitentiary|t=prison}}, being analyzed as an abbreviation (1884).<ref name="oetyld">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q858177}}An [[enclosure]] ([[enclose]]d area) used to contain [[domesticated]] animals, especially [[sheep]] or [[cattle]].
#: {{ux|en|There are two steers in the third '''pen'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[penitentiary]], i.e. a state or federal prison for convicted felons.
#: {{ux|en|They caught him with a stolen horse, and he wound up in the '''pen''' again.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1957|author=w:Stephen Sondheim|title=Gee, Officer Krupke|album=w:West Side Story|text=Officer Krupke, you've done it againǃ / This boy don't need a judge, he needs a year in the '''penǃ''' / It ain't just a question of misunderstood / Deep down inside him, he's no goodǃ}}
# {{senseid|en|Q579478}} {{lb|en|baseball}} The [[bullpen#English:_Q579478|bullpen]].
#: {{ux|en|Two righties are up in the '''pen'''.}}

