===Etymology 1===
From earlier {{m|en|peake}}, {{m|en|peek}}, {{m|en|peke}}, from {{inh|en|enm|pek}} (in place names), itself an alteration of {{m|enm|pike}}, {{m|enm|pyke}}, {{m|enm|pyk||a sharp point, pike}}, from {{inh|en|ang|pīc}}, {{m|ang|piic||a pike, needle, pin, peak, pinnacle}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*pīk}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*pīkaz||peak}}. 

Cognate with {{cog|nl|piek||pike, point, summit, peak}}, {{cog|da|pik||pike, peak}}, {{cog|sv|pik||pike, lance, point, peak}}, {{cog|no|pik||peak, summit}}. More at {{l|en|pike}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[point]]; the [[sharp]] [[end]] or [[top]] of anything that [[terminate]]s in a point; as, the peak, or front, of a [[cap]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|title=Joy of Cooking: All About Cookies|page=29|isbn=0743216806
|passage=A less risky method is to lift your whisk or beater to check the condition of the '''peaks''' of the egg whites; the foam should be just stiff enough to stand up in well-defined, unwavering '''peaks'''.}}
# The highest [[value]] reached by some [[quantity]] in a [[time period]].
#: {{syn|en|apex|pinnacle|Thesaurus:apex}}
#: {{ux|en|The stock market reached a '''peak''' in September 1929.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 23 2012|author=David Leonhardt|title=|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/10/24/us/politics/race-for-president-leaves-income-slump-in-shadows.html?_r=1&hp|journal=New York Times|accessdate=24 October 2012
|passage=By last year, family income was 8 percent lower than it had been 11 years earlier, at its '''peak''' in 2000, according to inflation-adjusted numbers from the Census Bureau.}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} The [[top]], or one of the tops, of a [[hill]], [[mountain]]{{,}} or [[range]], ending in a [[point]].
#: {{syn|en|summit|top}}
#: {{ux|en|They reached the '''peak''' after 8 hours of climbing.}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} The whole hill or mountain, especially when isolated.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1898|author=w:Arnold Henry Savage Landor|title=In the Forbidden Land|chapter=62
|passage=To the South we observed a large plain some ten miles wide, with snowy '''peaks''' rising on the farther side. In front was a hill projecting into the plain, on which stood a mani wall; and this latter discovery made me feel quite confident that I was on the high road to Lhassa.}}
# {{lb|en|headwear|UK}} [[visor]] {{gloss|horizontal part of a [[cap]] sticking out in front and shading the wearer's eyes}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The upper aftermost corner of a [[fore-and-aft]] sail.
#: {{ux|en|'''peak'''-halyards}}
#: {{ux|en|'''peak'''-brails}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[narrow]] part of a [[vessel]]'s [[bow]], or the [[hold]] within it.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[extremity]] of an [[anchor]] [[fluke]]; the [[bill]].
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} A local maximum of a function, e.g. for sine waves, each point at which the value of ''y'' is at its maximum.
# {{lb|en|euphemistic}} {{cap|orgasm}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2013-04-23|author=Crystal Green|title=Made in Texas!|publisher=Harlequin|isbn=9780373657414|page=214|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780373657414/page/n217/mode/1up?view=theater
|text=As she came to her '''peak''', he was with her all the way, making her crash, moan, say his name again and again.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|internet slang}} Something of [[exceptional]] quality.
#: {{ux|en|There's so much '''peak''' on this website!}}

